select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The ancient writer Herodotus called Egypt the gift of which river?","options":["the Tigris","the Nile","the Euphrates","the Jordan"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Egypt''s life depended on the Nile''s yearly flood, which Herodotus summed up in that famous phrase."},{"prompt":"Who is traditionally credited with unifying Upper and Lower Egypt around 3100 BCE?","options":["Khufu","Narmer (Menes)","Ramesses II","Cleopatra VII"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Narmer, also called Menes, is linked to the joining of the two lands and the start of the dynastic age."},{"prompt":"The Great Pyramid at Giza was built during the Old Kingdom for which pharaoh?","options":["Djoser","Khufu","Thutmose III","Tutankhamun"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Khufu of the Fourth Dynasty built the largest of the Giza pyramids around 2560 BCE."},{"prompt":"During the New Kingdom, which pharaoh promoted worship of a single sun disk, the Aten?","options":["Hatshepsut","Akhenaten","Ramesses II","Narmer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Akhenaten elevated the Aten above the other gods and moved the capital to Amarna."},{"prompt":"In funerary belief, the heart of the dead was weighed against the feather of which goddess of truth and order?","options":["Isis","Hathor","Ma''at","Nut"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A heart balanced against the feather of Ma''at could pass on to the afterlife."},{"prompt":"The Rosetta Stone helped scholars read hieroglyphs because it repeated one decree in hieroglyphic, demotic, and which other script?","options":["Latin","Greek","Hebrew","cuneiform"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scholars could still read the Greek version, which unlocked the hieroglyphic one."},{"prompt":"Egypt''s long independence ended when it became a Roman province after the death of which last pharaoh in 30 BCE?","options":["Nefertiti","Hatshepsut","Cleopatra VII","Isis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cleopatra VII of the Ptolemaic line died in 30 BCE, and Egypt passed to Rome."},{"prompt":"The dark, fertile silt left by the Nile''s flood led Egyptians to call their land Kemet, meaning the what?","options":["red land","black land","green land","white land"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kemet, the black land, was the floodplain soil; Deshret, the red land, was the desert."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The gift of the Nile: geography and agriculture', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why the ancient Greek writer Herodotus called Egypt the gift of the Nile, and describe how the river''s yearly flood shaped farming, settlement, and the calendar. By the end you should be able to place Upper and Lower Egypt correctly and connect the flood cycle to the three seasons of the Egyptian year. Warm-up: if nearly all of Egypt is rainless desert, where would you expect almost everyone to live, and why? Hold that answer in mind, because the whole story of this civilization follows from a single thin ribbon of water cutting through the sand. The river did not just support Egypt; it set the rhythm of work, worship, and government for three thousand years."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Egypt sits in the northeastern corner of Africa, where the Nile flows north through a narrow valley and fans out into a broad delta before reaching the Mediterranean Sea. Rain almost never falls, so life depended on the river. Each summer, roughly July to September, rains far to the south swelled the Nile until it spilled over its banks in an event called the inundation. When the water drained away it left a layer of dark, mineral-rich silt. Egyptians named their fertile floodplain Kemet, the black land, and the barren desert beyond it Deshret, the red land. The flood was so central that the civil year was split into three seasons: Akhet, the inundation; Peret, the growing season when fields were sown; and Shemu, the harvest. The Nile was also the main highway, carrying boats, grain, and stone. A good flood meant plenty, while a weak or violent one meant famine."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"The historian Herodotus, writing in the fifth century BCE, recorded the famous idea that Egypt was the gift of the river. Long before him, Egyptians had learned to work with the flood rather than against it. Farmers grew emmer wheat and barley, which they turned into bread and beer, the two staples of every table, along with flax for linen cloth. They built basins and canals to trap and spread the floodwater, a method known as basin irrigation, and lifted water to higher ground with a counterweighted pole called a shadoof. Officials measured the height of each flood using stone gauges called nilometers, because the reading predicted the size of the harvest and the taxes that could be collected. The split between Upper Egypt, the southern valley, and Lower Egypt, the northern delta, came straight from this geography and shaped politics for millennia."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which direction does the Nile flow? 2) What did Egyptians call the fertile black-soil region? 3) Name the three seasons of the Egyptian year. 4) What two foods were the grain staples? 5) Why was a nilometer reading useful to the government? Answers: 1) North, toward the Mediterranean Sea. 2) Kemet, the black land. 3) Akhet (inundation), Peret (growing), and Shemu (harvest). 4) Bread and beer, made from emmer wheat and barley. 5) It predicted the flood''s size, and therefore the coming harvest and the taxes that could be set. Try to say each answer aloud before checking, and notice how every one of them traces back to the behavior of the river itself."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the Nile flows south because south looks like down on a map. It flows north, which is exactly why Upper Egypt lies in the south and Lower Egypt in the north. Mistake: picturing the flood as a yearly disaster. A normal inundation was welcome and necessary, and only its failure brought hardship, since a missed flood meant no fresh silt and a thin harvest. Mistake: imagining Egyptians spread evenly across the land, when in fact they clustered along the river and in the delta where water reached. Exit: (a) Who called Egypt the gift of the Nile? (b) Which is farther south, Upper or Lower Egypt? Answers: Herodotus; Upper Egypt is farther south."}]}'::jsonb, null, 13),
  (3, 'Unification and the Early Dynastic Period', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how Upper and Lower Egypt became one kingdom around 3100 BCE and identify what the Early Dynastic Period, Dynasties 1 and 2, set in place. By the end you should recognize the double crown, know why Memphis mattered, and understand why this founding era, not the pyramid age, is where Egyptian kingship truly begins. Warm-up: if two rival regions merged under a single ruler, what symbols might that ruler adopt to show command over both at once? Keep that question in mind, because the Egyptians answered it with a crown, a capital city, and a style of art and writing that would endure for thousands of years almost unchanged."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Before unification, Egypt was two lands: Upper Egypt, the long southern valley, and Lower Egypt, the northern delta. Tradition and the archaeological record agree that around 3100 BCE a southern ruler brought the two together into a single state, opening the dynastic age that historians divide into roughly thirty numbered dynasties. The king of the united land wore a double crown, the pschent, combining the white crown of Upper Egypt with the red crown of Lower Egypt. A new capital, Memphis, rose near the point where valley meets delta, a strategic hinge between the regions. The Early Dynastic Period, covering the first two dynasties from about 3100 to 2686 BCE, built the institutions later ages inherited: a god-king at the center, a growing body of officials and scribes, royal tombs, and the early use of hieroglyphic writing to record ownership, taxes, and royal deeds."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"Unification is linked to a king the later Egyptians called Menes, usually identified with the ruler Narmer known from archaeology. The Narmer Palette, a carved stone slab, shows a king wearing the white crown of the south on one side and the red crown of the north on the other while striking down enemies; many scholars read it as a monument to the joining of the two lands. Memphis, protected by the god Ptah, became the administrative heart of the kingdom. During the first two dynasties, kings were buried at Abydos and later Saqqara in large mud-brick tombs called mastabas, flat-topped rectangular structures that were the direct ancestors of the pyramid. Writing, the conventions of art, and royal ritual all took recognizable Egyptian form in this founding period, giving the later pyramid builders a template to inherit."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Roughly what year marks the unification of Egypt? 2) What was the combined crown called, and which two crowns made it up? 3) Which city became the new capital? 4) Which king, also called Menes, is credited with unification? 5) What is a mastaba? Answers: 1) About 3100 BCE. 2) The pschent, combining the white crown of Upper Egypt and the red crown of Lower Egypt. 3) Memphis. 4) Narmer. 5) A flat-topped mud-brick tomb, the forerunner of the pyramid. Notice how each answer belongs to the founding period, before any pyramid was raised, which is a common point of confusion worth fixing now."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming the pyramids come first. The Early Dynastic Period precedes the Old Kingdom, and royal tombs in this era were still mastabas, not pyramids. Mistake: mixing up the crowns. The white crown belongs to Upper, or southern, Egypt, and the red crown to Lower, or northern, Egypt, and only the pschent joins them. Mistake: treating the thirty dynasties as evenly spaced reigns, when they are simply the scheme historians use to group ruling lines across very uneven stretches of time. Exit: (a) The Early Dynastic Period covers which two dynasties? (b) What does the Narmer Palette seem to commemorate? Answers: Dynasties 1 and 2; the unification of Upper and Lower Egypt under one king."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12),
  (4, 'The Old Kingdom and the pyramids', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why the Old Kingdom, Dynasties 3 to 6 from about 2686 to 2181 BCE, is called the Age of the Pyramids, and describe how Egyptians understood the pharaoh as a god-king. By the end you should be able to order the key pyramids, name their builders, and reject the myth that they were raised by mobs of foreign slaves. Warm-up: what would it take, in organization and resources, to build a single monument out of millions of precisely cut stone blocks? Thinking through that question is the fastest way to see why only a strong, centralized state at the height of its wealth could have produced the pyramids at all."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Old Kingdom was a time of strong central rule from Memphis, when the state could gather enormous labor and wealth for royal building. The pharaoh was not merely a king but a living god, the earthly embodiment of the falcon god Horus and a son of the sun god Ra. His duty was to uphold ma''at, the cosmic order of truth, balance, and justice, and his tomb was a device for securing his eternal life and, through him, the stability of Egypt itself. Pyramids were royal tombs; their shape may have evoked the rays of the sun or the mound of creation rising from the waters. Building them required quarrying, transport by river, a skilled permanent workforce, and seasonal laborers who were most likely fed and housed in nearby villages, not the legendary armies of enslaved foreigners."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"The first great leap was the Step Pyramid at Saqqara, built in the Third Dynasty for King Djoser around 2670 BCE by his architect Imhotep, who stacked six mastabas into the first monumental stone building known in the world. In the Fourth Dynasty, King Sneferu experimented with true smooth-sided pyramids, and his son Khufu built the Great Pyramid at Giza around 2560 BCE, the largest of all, standing originally about 146 meters tall. Khufu''s successors Khafre and Menkaure added the second and third Giza pyramids, and the Great Sphinx, a lion-bodied guardian with a royal head, is usually linked to Khafre. Religious spells carved inside later Old Kingdom pyramids, the Pyramid Texts, are among the oldest religious writings known. By the Sixth Dynasty, long reigns and the rising power of provincial governors weakened the crown."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which dynasties make up the Old Kingdom? 2) What was the pharaoh believed to be? 3) Who designed the Step Pyramid, and for which king? 4) Which pharaoh built the Great Pyramid at Giza? 5) Which king is usually linked to the Great Sphinx? Answers: 1) Dynasties 3 to 6, about 2686 to 2181 BCE. 2) A living god, tied to Horus and the sun god Ra and responsible for upholding ma''at. 3) Imhotep, for King Djoser. 4) Khufu. 5) Khafre. If you can also place the Step Pyramid before the Giza pyramids in time, you have the chronology that trips up most newcomers to this period."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: believing the pyramids were built by vast armies of slaves. The evidence points to organized crews of paid workers and seasonal labor drawn from the farming population during the flood months. Mistake: confusing the Step Pyramid with the Giza pyramids. Djoser''s stepped tomb came first in the Third Dynasty, and the smooth-sided Giza pyramids followed under the Fourth. Mistake: treating the pharaoh as just a political ruler, when Old Kingdom belief made him a living god whose tomb protected the order of the whole land. Exit: (a) What was the pharaoh''s central religious duty? (b) Name the three kings whose pyramids stand at Giza. Answers: to uphold ma''at, the cosmic order; Khufu, Khafre, and Menkaure."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14),
  (5, 'The Middle Kingdom', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe how Egypt recovered from collapse to form the Middle Kingdom, Dynasties 11 and 12 from about 2055 to 1650 BCE, and explain why this era is remembered as a classical age of literature and strong administration. By the end you should be able to name who reunified Egypt, where the capital moved, and how the hope of an afterlife spread beyond the king. Warm-up: after a stretch of disorder and civil strife, what would a new ruler most want to restore first, and how might he keep powerful local nobles from breaking away again? That tension between the crown and the provinces is the key to understanding this whole period."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Old Kingdom broke down into the First Intermediate Period, a time of weak central authority when provincial governors, the nomarchs, ruled their own districts and rival lines of kings competed. Order returned when a Theban ruler, Mentuhotep II of the Eleventh Dynasty, defeated his rivals and reunited the land around 2055 BCE, opening the Middle Kingdom. The Twelfth Dynasty kings who followed built a tightly run state, moved the capital to a new city near the Faiyum region called Itjtawy, and reformed the administration to keep ambitious nobles in check. They drained and farmed land in the Faiyum, pushed Egypt''s southern border deep into Nubia with a chain of mud-brick fortresses, and promoted the worship of Osiris, god of the dead, so that a proper afterlife became a hope for more than just the king."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"Mentuhotep II is remembered as the founder of the Middle Kingdom and built a striking terraced mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari near Thebes. Amenemhat I founded the Twelfth Dynasty and shifted the capital north to Itjtawy. Senusret III, also written Senwosret, was a forceful ruler who campaigned in Nubia and reduced the power of the old provincial governors, strengthening the crown at their expense. This period produced classic works of Egyptian literature, including the Tale of Sinuhe, the story of an official''s exile and return, which later scribes copied for centuries as a model of fine writing. The Middle Kingdom eventually weakened and gave way to the Second Intermediate Period, when rulers of Near Eastern origin known as the Hyksos took control of the delta and introduced the horse-drawn chariot and the composite bow to Egypt."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What period of disorder came before the Middle Kingdom? 2) Who reunified Egypt to begin the Middle Kingdom? 3) Where did the Twelfth Dynasty move the capital? 4) Which god''s worship spread as a hope of afterlife for ordinary people? 5) Who were the Hyksos? Answers: 1) The First Intermediate Period. 2) Mentuhotep II of the Eleventh Dynasty. 3) To Itjtawy, near the Faiyum. 4) Osiris, god of the dead. 5) Rulers of Near Eastern origin who seized the delta and brought the horse and chariot to Egypt. Notice how reunification, capital, religion, and foreign contact all mark this single period."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: imagining Egyptian history as one unbroken empire. It rose and fell in cycles, with intermediate periods of division between the great kingdoms. Mistake: assuming only kings expected an afterlife. In the Middle Kingdom the spread of Osiris worship offered the hope of eternal life far more widely than before. Mistake: picturing the Hyksos as simple invaders who destroyed Egypt, when they ruled part of it for a time and left behind lasting military technology that Egypt would later turn against its neighbors. Exit: (a) Which two dynasties make up the Middle Kingdom? (b) What new military technology did the Hyksos bring? Answers: Dynasties 11 and 12; the horse-drawn chariot."}]}'::jsonb, null, 13),
  (6, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The three seasons of the Egyptian agricultural year began with Akhet, which was the season of what?","options":["harvest","growing","inundation (flooding)","drought"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Akhet was the inundation; Peret was the growing season and Shemu the harvest."},{"prompt":"The Early Dynastic capital, founded near the junction of Upper and Lower Egypt, was which city?","options":["Thebes","Memphis","Alexandria","Amarna"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Memphis sat at the hinge between valley and delta and served as the early capital."},{"prompt":"The earliest large stone pyramid, the Step Pyramid at Saqqara, was designed by Imhotep for which Third Dynasty king?","options":["Khufu","Djoser","Sneferu","Menkaure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Imhotep stacked six mastabas into the Step Pyramid for King Djoser around 2670 BCE."},{"prompt":"Which king is generally credited with reunifying Egypt and beginning the Middle Kingdom after the First Intermediate Period?","options":["Mentuhotep II","Narmer","Ramesses II","Ahmose I"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mentuhotep II of the Eleventh Dynasty defeated his rivals and reunited the land around 2055 BCE."},{"prompt":"In the Old Kingdom, the pharaoh was regarded as what?","options":["an elected official","a living god linked to Horus and Ra","a foreign governor","a high priest only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pharaoh was seen as a living god, tied to Horus and Ra, who upheld ma''at."},{"prompt":"The Great Sphinx at Giza is most often associated with which Fourth Dynasty pharaoh, builder of the second pyramid?","options":["Khufu","Khafre","Menkaure","Djoser"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Sphinx is usually linked to Khafre, whose pyramid is the second at Giza."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (7, 'The New Kingdom and empire', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Trace Egypt at its most powerful during the New Kingdom, Dynasties 18 to 20 from about 1550 to 1077 BCE, and identify its most famous rulers and why each matters. By the end you should be able to connect the expulsion of the Hyksos to the rise of an empire, and place Hatshepsut, Thutmose III, Akhenaten, Tutankhamun, and Ramesses II in order and in context. Warm-up: how might expelling a foreign occupier change a country''s view of its army and its borders? That hard-won militarization is exactly what turned an inward-looking Egypt into an empire reaching from Nubia in the south to Syria in the north."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The New Kingdom began when Ahmose I drove the Hyksos out of the delta and founded the Eighteenth Dynasty around 1550 BCE. Hardened by that war and armed with chariots and composite bows, Egypt turned outward and built an empire stretching from Nubia to the Levant. Pharaohs now led armies in person, and conquered regions sent tribute to Thebes, which grew rich as the empire''s religious capital under the god Amun-Ra. Royal burial changed too: instead of pyramids, kings were laid in hidden rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings across the river from Thebes, hoping to foil the robbers who had plundered earlier monuments. This was an age of wealth, vast temple building, diplomacy among the great powers of the day, and some of the most famous names in all of Egyptian history."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"Hatshepsut, one of the few women to rule as pharaoh, from about 1479 to 1458 BCE, sponsored a famous trading expedition to the land of Punt and built an elegant terraced temple at Deir el-Bahari. Thutmose III, sometimes called the Napoleon of Egypt, expanded the empire to its greatest extent and won the Battle of Megiddo. Akhenaten, about 1353 to 1336 BCE, launched a religious revolution, promoting the sun disk Aten above all other gods, moving the capital to a new city at Amarna, and inspiring an unusually naturalistic art style. His successor Tutankhamun reversed those reforms and restored the worship of Amun; his nearly intact tomb, found in 1922, made him world famous. In the Nineteenth Dynasty, Ramesses II, called the Great, reigned about sixty-six years, fought the Hittites at the Battle of Kadesh around 1274 BCE, agreed one of the earliest recorded peace treaties, and built the temples at Abu Simbel."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Who expelled the Hyksos and founded the Eighteenth Dynasty? 2) Where were New Kingdom pharaohs buried instead of in pyramids? 3) Which female pharaoh sent an expedition to Punt? 4) What religious change did Akhenaten make? 5) Which pharaoh fought at Kadesh and built Abu Simbel? Answers: 1) Ahmose I. 2) The Valley of the Kings, near Thebes. 3) Hatshepsut. 4) He elevated the sun disk Aten above the other gods and moved the capital to Amarna. 5) Ramesses II. If you can also explain why pyramids gave way to hidden tombs, you have grasped how fear of robbery reshaped royal burial."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: still picturing pyramids as royal tombs in this era. New Kingdom kings used hidden rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings, not pyramids. Mistake: thinking Tutankhamun was a mighty ruler because he is so famous today. His fame comes almost entirely from his intact tomb, not from his short and relatively minor reign. Mistake: calling Akhenaten''s religion a full monotheism like later faiths, when it was a sharp but short-lived elevation of one god that his successors soon undid. Exit: (a) Which god dominated New Kingdom Thebes? (b) What was unusual about Akhenaten''s religion? Answers: Amun, often Amun-Ra; he promoted the single sun disk Aten above the traditional gods."}]}'::jsonb, null, 15),
  (8, 'Religion and the afterlife', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe the main Egyptian gods, explain why and how the dead were mummified, and say what the Book of the Dead was for. By the end you should be able to connect the belief that the soul needed a preserved body to the practical steps of embalming, and to describe the weighing of the heart. Warm-up: if you believed your body had to survive in order for your soul to live on, how would you treat the dead, and what might you place in the tomb to help them on their way? Egyptian funerary religion is simply the elaborate, careful answer to that one powerful question."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Egyptian religion was polytheistic, with many gods who governed nature and human life. Chief among them were Ra, the sun god; Osiris, lord of the dead and the underworld; Isis, his wife, a goddess of magic and motherhood; their son Horus, the falcon god tied to kingship; Anubis, the jackal-headed god of embalming; and Thoth, the ibis-headed god of writing and wisdom. Above all stood the principle of ma''at, truth and cosmic order, which both gods and kings were bound to uphold. Egyptians believed a person had several spiritual parts, including the ka, a life force, and the ba, a kind of personality, which needed the preserved body as a home. The goal of funerary religion was to pass safely through judgment into the afterlife and live on in a paradise pictured as the Field of Reeds."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"To preserve the body, embalmers removed the internal organs, storing several in containers called canopic jars, then dried the corpse with a natural salt, natron, over about forty days before wrapping it in linen; the full process traditionally took around seventy days. The brain was usually discarded, but the heart was deliberately left in place, because it was thought to be the seat of thought and would be judged. In the hall of judgment, Anubis weighed the dead person''s heart against the feather of Ma''at: a heart as light as the feather passed on to Osiris, while a heavy, guilt-laden heart was devoured by a waiting monster. To guide the dead through these trials, scribes wrote collections of spells on papyrus, known today as the Book of the Dead, and placed them in the tomb along with food, goods, and small servant figures called shabtis."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the god of the dead and the underworld. 2) Which god, with a jackal''s head, oversaw embalming? 3) What were canopic jars for? 4) Against what was the heart weighed in judgment? 5) What was the Book of the Dead? Answers: 1) Osiris. 2) Anubis. 3) Storing internal organs removed during mummification. 4) The feather of Ma''at, goddess of truth and order. 5) A collection of spells written on papyrus to help the dead reach the afterlife. If you can also explain why the heart alone was left in the body, you have understood what Egyptians thought judgment actually tested."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking the heart was removed like the other organs. It was left in the body precisely so it could be weighed in judgment. Mistake: treating the Book of the Dead as a single fixed scripture, when it was a varying collection of spells selected and copied for each individual. Mistake: assuming mummification was only for pharaohs, when over time wealthier Egyptians of many ranks sought it too, within their means. Exit: (a) What natural salt dried the body? (b) Which spiritual parts needed the preserved body as a home? Answers: natron; the ka and the ba. Keep the sequence in mind: organs out, body dried with natron, wrapped in linen, then judged."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14),
  (9, 'Writing, knowledge, and daily life', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain the Egyptian writing systems and how hieroglyphs were finally read again after being forgotten, and describe the layers of Egyptian society and everyday life. By the end you should be able to distinguish hieroglyphic, hieratic, and demotic, explain the role of the Rosetta Stone, and sketch the social pyramid from pharaoh to farmer. Warm-up: if a script falls completely out of use for more than a thousand years, what would you need in order to decode it again? The answer, a long document repeating the same text in a language you can already read, is exactly what eventually unlocked ancient Egypt for the modern world."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Egyptians used several scripts. Hieroglyphs were the formal, picture-based signs carved on temples and monuments; hieratic was a faster, cursive form used by scribes on papyrus; and demotic was an even quicker everyday script that developed later. Writing was recorded on papyrus, a paper-like material made by pressing together strips cut from the papyrus reed that grew in the delta marshes. Only a small, trained minority, the scribes, could read and write, which made them powerful and respected, since literacy opened the door to government service. Beneath the god-king stood a pyramid of society: viziers and high officials, then priests, scribes, and soldiers, then skilled artisans, and, forming the great majority, the farmers who worked the land, with servants and the enslaved at the bottom."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"Knowledge of how to read hieroglyphs was lost for centuries after the old Egyptian religion faded away. The key to recovering it was the Rosetta Stone, a slab carrying a single priestly decree issued in 196 BCE and written three ways: in hieroglyphic, in demotic, and in Greek. Because scholars could still read Greek, the stone, found in 1799, let the French scholar Jean-Francois Champollion crack the hieroglyphic system in 1822. Daily life for most Egyptians centered on the farming year and the river. People lived in mud-brick houses, wore light linen clothing against the heat, and ate bread, beer, vegetables, and fish. Family life was central, and Egyptian women held notable legal rights for the ancient world: they could own and inherit property, run businesses, and seek divorce, options denied to women in many neighboring societies."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three Egyptian scripts described here. 2) What was papyrus made from? 3) Who were the only people who could usually read and write? 4) Why was the Rosetta Stone so useful? 5) Name one legal right Egyptian women held. Answers: 1) Hieroglyphic, hieratic, and demotic. 2) Strips cut from the papyrus reed. 3) The scribes. 4) It repeated the same decree in Greek, which scholars could read, alongside the hieroglyphic and demotic versions. 5) They could own and inherit property, run a business, or seek divorce, any one of these. If you can also name Champollion and the year 1822, you have the decisive moment of rediscovery."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking hieroglyphs are purely pictures standing for whole words. The system also used signs for sounds, which is exactly why it could spell out foreign names and why the Greek names on the Rosetta Stone gave scholars their way in. Mistake: assuming most Egyptians were literate, when in fact reading and writing were rare skills held mainly by trained scribes. Mistake: picturing Egyptian women as powerless, when their legal rights were unusually strong for the ancient world. Exit: (a) Who deciphered hieroglyphs, and in what year? (b) Which group formed the majority of Egyptian society? Answers: Jean-Francois Champollion, in 1822; the farmers."}]}'::jsonb, null, 13),
  (10, 'Art, architecture, decline, and legacy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize the conventions of Egyptian art and temple architecture, and trace Egypt''s long decline through the Late Period and Greco-Roman rule to its lasting legacy. By the end you should be able to explain why figures were drawn the way they were, name the great temple at Karnak, and follow Egypt from the Late Period to Alexander, the Ptolemies, Cleopatra, and Rome. Warm-up: why might a civilization keep the same artistic style for thousands of years almost unchanged? The answer, that the art was made for eternity rather than for a lifelike moment, is the key to reading Egyptian images and monuments correctly."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Egyptian art followed strict conventions meant to last for eternity rather than to capture a single passing moment. Figures were usually shown with the head and legs in profile but the eye and shoulders from the front, so that each part appeared in its clearest view, and more important people were drawn larger, a device called hierarchy of scale. Temples were built in stone to endure, approached through massive gateways called pylons, lined with columns, and marked by tall obelisks. The temple complex of Karnak at Thebes, dedicated chiefly to Amun-Ra and expanded by many pharaohs over centuries, is among the largest religious structures ever built, famous for the forest of giant columns in its great hypostyle hall. Such monuments proclaimed the power of the gods and the king, and because they were meant to stand forever, a remarkable amount of them survives today."},{"heading":"Key events & figures","text":"After the New Kingdom, Egypt entered a long, uneven decline through the Third Intermediate Period and then the Late Period, from 664 to 332 BCE, which included rule by Nubian kings and conquest by the Persians. In 332 BCE Alexander the Great took Egypt and founded the city of Alexandria; after his death his general Ptolemy began the Greek-speaking Ptolemaic dynasty. Its last ruler, Cleopatra VII, allied with the Roman leaders Julius Caesar and then Mark Antony, but after defeat by Octavian she died in 30 BCE, and Egypt became a province of Rome. Yet Egypt''s legacy endured. Its monuments, its mathematics and medicine, its 365-day calendar, and its art influenced the Greeks, the Romans, and later ages, and the decipherment of hieroglyphs eventually reopened its three-thousand-year record to the modern world."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Describe how the human figure was typically drawn in Egyptian art. 2) What is hierarchy of scale? 3) Which huge temple complex at Thebes honored Amun-Ra? 4) Who conquered Egypt in 332 BCE and founded Alexandria? 5) Who was the last pharaoh, and when did Egypt become Roman? Answers: 1) With the head and legs in profile and the eye and shoulders from the front. 2) Drawing more important figures larger than less important ones. 3) Karnak. 4) Alexander the Great. 5) Cleopatra VII; Egypt became a Roman province in 30 BCE. If you can also place the Late Period before Alexander, you have the full arc from independence to Roman rule."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: judging Egyptian art as clumsy for ignoring realistic perspective, when it deliberately combined the clearest view of each body part to make an image fit for eternity. Mistake: assuming Cleopatra was Egyptian by descent, when she belonged to the Greek-speaking Ptolemaic line founded after Alexander and ruled centuries after the pharaohs of the pyramids. Mistake: imagining Egypt simply vanished, when its calendar, learning, and art passed into later civilizations. Exit: (a) Name one feature of monumental temple architecture. (b) In what year did Cleopatra''s death bring Egypt under Rome? Answers: pylons, columns, obelisks, or a hypostyle hall, any one; 30 BCE."}]}'::jsonb, null, 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a' and l.position = v.pos
);
